						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2739858)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2739858">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818412" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1486159862 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Feb 2017 22:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2739858">
									

<p>Well, granted, you and I are both established authors here and this sort of stuff effectively seems pretty easy to us. I don't reference How to Write an SCP anymore when I write my new articles, but for the average new writer who's never tried this sort of thing before, all the guides and things to remember that get thrown around can be pretty overwhelming.</p>
<p>Besides, I don't think any of the guides really give a straightforward step-by-step rundown of the writing process.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a) they shouldn't be here, b) this isn't going to do them much better, c) they're not going to be willing to put in the effort of making a good article.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels&#8230; kind of elitist, to be honest. Also, in the case of plenty of the collabs I've written with first-time authors, people have benefited a lot more from having some form of process guidance (e.g., think flowchart not encyclopedia) rather than just being directed to a guide full of details that aren't all immediately relevant to what they want to do.</p>
<p>I do agree that this should have links to further reading, though. I'll nudge Roget about that.</p>

							</div>

								
