														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2746034">
									

<p>I don't see CWW claiming they suck (although I can't speak for them), just the complaint we see on all new guides that there is no evidence that the current abundance of guides/essays are improving coldposts/help new authors, so why would <em>more</em> guides be the answer?</p>
<p>It's legitimate to question progress towards goals, and the usability and uptake of these guides are among those. If we're just trying to present alternative writing methods for the curious and self-reflective, they're great. If we're trying to guide-bomb the less-skilled among the newbies into being better writers, who knows?</p>
<p>(I honestly don't know that I can say that any of these broad writing guides have changed my writing technique since I joined. I learned much more from reading SCPs and comments/critiques than I ever have with these. This is anecdotal, but it's what I've got.)</p>
<p>I also honestly can't think of how to actually answer this question, unless wikidot tracks page views in addition to votes, or we survey users on actual guide use.</p>
<p>As for this essay on a content level, no vote. The basic elements are well spelled out, but it treats the key abstract idea of SCP=story (and the language in general) very literally and low-fidelity, and I have no idea if that take is any more clear on the matter for new writers.</p>

							</div>

								
